{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED. Round-2 design review of the REVISED SUPPORTED-HOST DECISION, proposal 8a758fb795b459f78832efa3ca9aee02c226a6d2, against my round-1 verdict 105cefd3 and against main 23c4c759. Design review of a log record: no head, no artifact, read-only. Both defects I called blocking in round 1 are closed. The residuals below are wording precision and belong to the implementing request, not to this decision.\n\nTRUTHFULNESS OF THE SUPPORT CLAIM. Yes. The decision paragraph supports the signing resident \"only where every process that can reach it or read its repository key storage is trusted as the same operator\" and refuses shared-host and mutually untrusted local-process support outright. That is the boundary the code actually has, and the revision now draws it around the copy paths as well as the port. The deployment paragraph refuses the three inferences an implementer would otherwise make - generic loopback, generic containerization, file mode alone - and requires the startup, status, browser and MCP surfaces to say plainly that every process inside the boundary can act as every actor key the application can open. Nothing in the record claims a property the code does not have.\n\nDISPOSITION OF THE NINE ROUND-1 ITEMS, in the numbering of the round-2 request.\n\n1. Compromise window. CLOSED. The observed-boundary paragraph now states it directly: \"A client can renew the identifier indefinitely; the lease expires only when renewals stop, and departure or resident restart ends it.\" Re-verified: AnnounceSessionIdentity at internal/nexus/nexus.go:317-328 refuses only a different actor or fingerprint and re-authenticates nothing, and announceFor writes expiresAt of now plus ttl on every renewal at internal/nexus/nexus.go:330-372. The false self-healing sentence is gone.\n\n2. Hygiene claim. CLOSED. The revision separates description from aspiration. Today is described honestly - \"current hygiene is incomplete: the departure URL contains the private identifier, MCP whoami exposes it to tool transcripts, and HTTP errors may include request URLs\" - and the prohibition now sits in the credential-correction paragraph as a requirement on a future minted credential, extended to referrers, query strings, path segments, tool transcripts and returned URL errors, which is the wider list I asked for. All three admitted leaks re-verified at this main: the departure route carries the session in the path at internal/service/server.go:107 and 415-419, both clients send it that way at ui/src/lib/api.ts:309 and cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1199, whoami returns the live session at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:809 and 828 under the description at :545, and TransportError at internal/residentclient/client.go:70-73 wraps and preserves the url.Error built at :246.\n\n3. Named form of the operator acknowledgement. PARTIALLY. \"unless its operator deliberately acknowledges trusted local resident clients\" does close the loophole I named, because a pre-acknowledged shipped default is neither the operator nor deliberate, and fail-closed makes absence a refusal rather than an inference. But no form is named, so two implementations can both satisfy this sentence with different acts. The implementing request should settle the form - a per-invocation flag, or a config key the operator or its automation must write by name.\n\n4. Scope of the gate. CLOSED. \"This acknowledgement discloses only the resident boundary; it cannot make the host safe, protect direct gs CLI key access, or turn same-account malware into a trusted client.\" That is the disclosure-not-control framing I asked for, and it names the CLI path explicitly. Re-verified that the path is real and needs no resident: Workspace.Act at internal/app/app.go:709-719 loads any configured actor's private key and signs through BuildActRequest.\n\n5. Backups and copies. CLOSED. The storage paragraph puts \"the OS account, repository common directory, backups, snapshots, developer tooling and sync services that can copy those files\" inside the supported boundary, requires keys to stay out of unprotected backups and shared sync, and notes that protected copies expand the documented recovery boundary. The closing criteria repeat it as \"outside unauthorized storage and backups\".\n\n6. Resident must mint. CLOSED. \"The resident, not the client, must mint the private session credential after accepting the actor selection inside the acknowledged trust boundary. It must use system randomness with a fixed minimum entropy and validated opaque shape.\" That is exactly the criterion that was missing. See new defect N3 for the one residual.\n\n7. Wrong custody package. CLOSED. internal/custody no longer appears. The record now says \"Repository actor keys are application-host custody managed through internal/app and local key files above the kernel\", which is where custody actually lives. Note for the implementing request, not a defect in this record: docs/reference/architecture.md:316 still describes internal/custody as managing local keys and migrations above the kernel, which is inaccurate against this main and is where the original proposal inherited the wrong name.\n\n8. Hostile web page reaching loopback. CLOSED, and better than named. Rather than only crediting the existing guard, the revision makes it a contract term: \"Mutating HTTP endpoints must reject hostile web-page and cross-origin use before side effects through an explicit Origin/Host/content-type policy with no permissive CORS path.\" A required property cannot be removed as redundant, which was my concern. The implementation it describes exists and still matches at internal/service/server.go:594-612.\n\n9. Layer naming. PARTIALLY. The architecture sentence now identifies the right components - nexus session binding, service and MCP transport, kernel verification and fold semantics unchanged - so a reader is sent to the right code. The term is still imprecise: \"application-host\" collides with layer 4, Application host binding, which is interpreter selection at docs/reference/architecture.md:146 and has nothing to do with transport trust or key custody, and \"outer-adapter\" is not the page's term either; the package table at :320 calls internal/app the \"Application host and boundary adapter\", and the surfaces are layer 7 at :265. Naming layers 3 and 7 and internal/app would remove the ambiguity. Not blocking.\n\nSPOT-CHECK OF THE SIX STRUCTURAL CLAIMS ROUND 1 CONFIRMED. Five survive the edit unchanged and remain accurate: loopback-only listeners, bind-once then bearer use by /v0/say and /v0/act, resident-side signing with the selected actor key, handles unrelated to the bearer value and display-only, expiry on departure or restart, and any local process reaching the port being able to announce any actor whose key files internal/app can open. I re-confirmed that no reverse handle lookup exists - presence is keyed by session identifier at internal/nexus/nexus.go and HandleFor at :541 has no inverse - so \"display-only and never authorizes an act\" holds. The sixth is narrowed incorrectly; see N1. The revision also adopts the resolver gap I reported as evidence-check item (a): \"The listener must resolve localhost and require every resolved address to be loopback, rather than trusting a hostname spelling.\" Still warranted - validateLoopbackListen at cmd/gs/main.go:1751-1764 returns nil for the literal hostname without resolving it.\n\nNEW DEFECTS INTRODUCED BY THE REVISION. None blocking; all four are corrections to wording.\n\nN1. \"Current MCP clients choose an actor and a private session identifier when announcing presence\" understates the surface by naming only the adapter. The browser UI chooses its own identifier too: mintSessionID at ui/src/lib/session.ts:16-20 draws nine random bytes and prefixes \"web:\", and useSession announces it at :30-40 through api.announce at ui/src/lib/api.ts:303-308. The browser is also the client whose identifier reaches a URL path, at ui/src/lib/api.ts:309, and the client a hostile page is nearest to. Say current clients, browser UI and MCP adapter.\n\nN2. The compromise paragraph reads as description but names a control that does not exist. \"A leaked live session compromises its bound actor until revocation, departure, lapse without renewal or restart\" - there is no revocation today; internal/nexus offers Depart at :495-505 and expire at :513-523 and nothing else. Explicit revocation is required of the future credential in the same record, which is correct, but here it sits in a sentence about what a leak costs now. Mark it as the future property, or drop it from this sentence. \"Lapse without renewal\" is likewise only an end condition if the holder stops renewing, which the observed-boundary paragraph states correctly two paragraphs earlier.\n\nN3. \"A fixed minimum entropy\" is unquantified, which leaves the residual of the defect it closes. Nine bytes, what the UI mints today, satisfies the sentence as written. The repository already sets a precedent worth naming: mintHandle at internal/nexus/nexus.go:73-96 draws sixteen bytes from crypto/rand for exactly this reason. Name the number.\n\nN4. Recommendation, not a defect in the record. The three current disclosures the revision now admits are live in main and this decision creates no work to repair them; the obligation is carried only as a property of a future credential that may be built much later. Whoami handing a live bearer credential to an agent transcript that routinely leaves the host is the sharpest of them, and the missing url.PathEscape at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1199 is a one-line fix. Per AGENTS.md, follow-up work begins with a durable request; filing one against this decision would give those repairs an owner.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers, per docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 3, nexus and live runtime, implemented by internal/nexus and hosted by internal/service; layer 7, the outer surfaces cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp and ui/ with the internal/service composition; and internal/app, the application host and boundary adapter at :320, which is where actor key custody sits. The record preserves the contract of every layer it does not touch, and I verified rather than assumed the kernel claim: signing remains ed25519 over the canonical intent built by BuildActRequest from Workspace.Act at internal/app/app.go:709-725, and nothing the record requires reaches the kernel or the fold. The acknowledgement is a startup precondition in cmd/gs, and the credential change is confined to internal/nexus and internal/service. No architecture page update is required by this record: it is a decision with no head and it changes no code contract. The implementing head will change the gs serve contract and must update that page in the same head, and should correct the internal/custody row at :316 while there.\n\nSECURITY. This review is the security analysis. The trust boundary is one OS account: loopback TCP that locates but does not identify a client, authority carried by a bearer string the client currently chooses, and a resident holding read access to every actor key file in the repository. The revision describes that accurately and now draws the boundary around backups, snapshots and sync as well, which is where the keys most easily leave a trusted host. Its four security requirements are each an improvement on the present state and each is warranted by code I checked: resident-minted credentials against the unconstrained client choice at internal/nexus/nexus.go:86-89; resolve-then-check against the hostname shortcut at cmd/gs/main.go:1756-1758; the origin and content-type contract against a hostile page reaching the port, matching internal/service/server.go:594-612; and the disclosure list against the three live leaks in item 2. Unresolved exposures remain in main and are named honestly by the record but owned by nobody, per N4. Defences that exist, that the record does not name, and that a later simplification should not remove: the global and per-actor session caps at internal/nexus/nexus.go:336-347, the two-minute TTL cap at internal/service/server.go:405-408, inbox deletion on identifier reuse at internal/nexus/nexus.go:348-350, which stops a recycled identifier inheriting an earlier recipient's frames, and route-template-only telemetry in internal/observe, which is why the identifier in the departure URL does not also reach the collector. No security defect in the record blocks approval.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. The revision is materially simpler than what it replaces and took both cuts I asked for: the ten numbered acceptance criteria are one prose paragraph, the nine-step recovery chain is one sentence, and the criterion that was a list of package names is now a testable condition about adversarial separate-process and separate-principal tests that also requires stating honestly whether same-account malware stays out of scope. The deferral of authenticated local transport is preserved and should stay deferred: a capability file readable by the same account defends against nothing a same-account process can not already do, so building a partial authentication framework would add structure and no property. Two small cuts remain, both in N2 and N3: the compromise sentence lists four end conditions where restart and the holder stopping carry the meaning, and \"fixed minimum entropy\" is longer and weaker than a number. Neither weakens a condition of satisfaction if cut. I found nothing else to remove without losing a load-bearing term.\n\nCONCLUSION. Approved as a design decision. The two blocking corrections from 105cefd3 are closed, five more are closed outright, two are partially closed in wording only, and the four new findings are corrections a ratifier can accept as amendments or hand to the implementing request. I would not hold the decision for any of them."}